WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.507
Dijkstra's algorithm finds single-source shortest paths when every edge weight is zero or positive.

00:00:06.507 --> 00:00:11.436
Initialize the source distance to zero and all other distances to infinity.

00:00:11.436 --> 00:00:18.600
Because weights are non-negative, the closest unfinalized vertex on the frontier can never be improved later.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.716
Use Python's heapq module to store candidate pairs of distance and vertex.

00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:31.422
Because heapq has no decrease-key operation, push a fresh tuple whenever a shorter path is found.

00:00:31.422 --> 00:00:37.920
Immediately discard stale heap entries whose popped distance exceeds the current best dist[u].

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:46.261
For each outgoing edge from u to v with weight w, compare d plus w against dist[v].

00:00:46.261 --> 00:00:54.056
Only update dist[v] and push to the heap when the new distance is strictly smaller.

00:00:54.056 --> 00:01:02.227
This strict check prevents duplicate heap pushes and keeps runtime at O((V + E) log V).

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.218
Many contest problems allow K discounted edges, parity switches, or fuel states along the route.

00:01:09.218 --> 00:01:16.304
Encode the extra parameter directly into the vertex state and run standard Dijkstra on the expanded graph.

00:01:16.304 --> 00:01:21.051
Store a parent array whenever you need to reconstruct the winning path.
